  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nThere is a cold front off to the northwest of the CWA in Iowa this \nmorning. It will dip down into central IL today. There could be some \nsprinkles (north of I-72) or light rain showers (near and south of I-\n72) this afternoon. Chances for showers are greatest tomorrow into \ntomorrow night, near and north of I-72 (40-70% chance). More light \nrain chances remain in the forecast through the weekend as the \nfrontal boundary lingers in the region. Fortunately, trick or \ntreating (or other fall activities) hours on Saturday look to be \nduring one of the lulls in precip chances in the forecast. Whatever \nlight rain manages to fall between now and Sunday will do little to \nalleviate the ongoing dry conditions.\n\nThe start of November will usher in quite a significant pattern \nchange. The synoptic models continue to develop a large, cutoff, \ndeepening upper low over the central Plains early next week, with an \nassociated intensifying surface low. This system will bring the \ngreatest chances for showers and thunderstorms to the area within \nthe next week. The NBM has a widespread 50-70% chance across central \nand southeastern IL focused on Monday evening into Tuesday evening. \nAnomalous PWATs for November are forecast for this system's passage, \nvalues 1.5-1.8 inches on various models. The LREF shows a 50-70% \nchance for over 1 inch of rain to fall in total through Wednesday \nmorning west of I-57.\n\nTemperatures will continue to be above normal; however, will trend \nslightly cooler starting Saturday into next week. Temperatures will \nbe in the upper 60s to low 70s for highs, which is still above \nnormal. The CPC's 6-10 day, 8-14 day, and Week 3-4 outlooks are all \ncontinuing to show above normal temperatures. The additional cloud \ncover and increased rain chances this weekend into next week may \ninhibit the temperatures from getting too warm, potentially being \ncooler than what is in the forecast now.",
